  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "No apparent truncation or garbling; the text presents the paper and appendices.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study reports downstream-task scores for graph foundation model variants at different parameter scales, but does not measure held-out language-model pretraining loss or fit scaling laws.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports graph-model downstream performance by model size, which bears on V6. Its figures about performance versus pretraining-data size and pretraining time do not report numerical values in the text, and the sensitivity plots are not scaling-law tests. No result measures held-out cross-entropy loss, fitted power laws, compute-optimal parameter/token allocations, or architectural shape effects.",
    "test_reason": "This is a graph foundation model (GNN plus vector-quantised codebook) method paper. It invokes 'GFM scaling laws' as a design premise and reports downstream accuracy for three of its own variants (5M, 10M, 60M). The variants differ in architecture as well as size: tiny has one codebook and no gating, base has 6 gated codebooks, large has 64 codebooks of 1024 vectors and a different load loss. Size is therefore confounded with architecture. There are only three points, no loss measure and no fit. The systems are not language models, so no row measures what V6 names (language-model downstream scaling). Nor do the rows show V5's power-law form. The record's V6 linkage and labels are dropped; the finding goes under fits_no_version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The authors claim MoT shows 'remarkable adherence to GFM scaling laws' with 'consistent' gains from tiny to large. They say large peaks on 6 of 8 fine-tuning tasks and 15 of 16 few-shot settings, but Tables 1–2 show large best on only 4 of 8 fine-tuning tasks and fewer few-shot settings. They acknowledge that large sometimes trails base and that limited pretraining data constrains larger variants.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Graph foundation model downstream evaluations across three MoT parameter sizes, rather than language-model held-out-loss sweeps, power-law fitting, or compute-optimal allocation experiments.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Downstream scores differ by dataset and are not uniformly monotonic across the three model sizes. The paper also says larger models can have diminished performance returns when pretraining data is insufficient.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Three MoT graph-model variants (5M/10M/60M) differ in codebook count and routing. Fine-tuning scores are non-monotonic in size: large is best on 4 of 8 tasks, base on 3 (WikiCS, WN18RR, MUV), and tiny on Reddit. Fig. 4 shows performance rising with pretraining data size (0.5–2 GB), with no numbers given.",
        "proposed_claim": "Downstream performance of graph foundation models rises with parameter or pretraining-data scale.",
